    "q-53225": {
      "dom_id": "step-q53225",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14237394/assets/0e112d00ddcb34c30a490d1a71e3e0b4a2c698707e3890666ac4e299c3ca37cd.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $∠BOA$ is a straight angle,  and $\\overset{\\to }{OD}$ is the bisector of $∠COA.$ If $m∠BOD={107}^{∘},$ find $m∠BOC.$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14237394/assets/2d75ac364d71d8dd6cf35cfe7f8966b603c3cbee0bc5adff5f7dadf7468bab4c.png)\n\nSince the angles $∠DOA$ and $∠BOD$ are supplementary, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠DOA & ={180}^{∘}-m∠BOD \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{107}^{∘} \\\\  & ={73}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Therefore,\n\n$$\nm∠DOA=m∠COD={73}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, by the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOC & =m∠BOD-m∠COD \\\\  & ={107}^{∘}-{73}^{∘} \\\\  & ={34}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
