    "q-117152": {
      "dom_id": "step-q117152",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14237394/assets/187b52aa0450e940bd644f9d1ae560a735c7e10e18157ecba910a6a817c11797.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $∠BOA$ is a straight angle,  and $\\overset{\\to }{OC}$ is the bisector of $∠BOD.$ If $m∠DOA={58}^{∘},$ find $m∠COA.$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14237394/assets/9d7c5789c579b9292beb17ad03ae6239083ffa70fe5ec581da33cd488e365344.png)\n\nSince the angles $∠BOD$ and $∠DOA$ are supplementary, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOD & ={180}^{∘}-m∠DOA \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{58}^{∘} \\\\  & ={122}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COD & =\\frac{m∠BOD}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{122}^{∘}}{2} \\\\  & ={61}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COA & =m∠COD+m∠DOA \\\\  & ={61}^{∘}+{58}^{∘} \\\\  & ={119}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
